WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.380
SPEAKER: Welcome back.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.460
In this video, we want to share
the approach to an API product

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.390
mindset and give you examples
on how to design your first API

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:14.500
product.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.130
Let's get started.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.440
I often get this question
from my customers

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.050
at the start of an engagement.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.030
What is an API?

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.840
The formal definition of API
is defined as application

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.610
programming interface.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.510
It is a way for systems to
communicate with each other.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.400
It leverages and reuses data,
functions, and applications,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.030
and combines them into new
business opportunities.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.280
And furthermore, it
connects consumers

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.980
of data, systems,
and applications

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.320
with providers of the same.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.450
Now that you have the
formal definition,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.440
you are probably
asking yourself,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.890
how does this
affect my business?

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.980
In the next few slides, we will
discuss a recommended approach

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.280
to designing your APIs, making
it easier for the developer

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.040
to discover and
begin using your APIs

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.400
in order to implement their
application with speed.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.730
So now, you may ask,
what is an API product

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:15.100
and why is it important
to my business?

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.400
Approaching an API with the
consumer experience in mind

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.010
is the cornerstone
of an API product.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.130
We call this an API-first
approach or outside

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.210
in thinking.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.830
Let me give you an example.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.710
For those of you who
are MBA fans, when

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.140
you think of Steph
Curry's basketball shoes,

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.990
you might envision a
cool blue and white shoe

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.260
that enables you to jump
higher and farther than anyone

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.550
else on the court.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.550
You might envision yourself
shooting 3-point baskets

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.470
from midcourt.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.850
Now, the shoe doesn't
really do that,

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.350
But it creates the vision of
someone doing awesome things

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.090
when they wear that shoe.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.400
You want to build
the same vision

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.740
or need with an API product.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.690
With a focus on the
consumer experience,

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.020
the API product can encompass
a similar vision and experience

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.620
for an API.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.070
Let's dig into how this is done.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.110
To understand the need
for an API product,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.170
you must first
understand the user.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.780
Let's start with asking
four key questions

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.810
centered around the
user of your API.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.410
Who is the user?

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.110
What are the user's needs?

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.880
Why do they need it?

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.160
When do they need it?

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.710
The answer to the first
question is simple.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.530
The user of your API is
an application developer

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.680
who will create
applications using your API.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.550
The application developer is
also known as the API consumer.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.930
Let me give you an example.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.020
A lottery company
in the UK wanted

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.740
to make purchasing a lottery
ticket easy for their players.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.960
Their goal was to give
everyone the ability

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.560
to make a purchase
anytime, anywhere.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.255
However, there were
some regulations

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:54.130
that needed to be met
before the player could

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.190
purchase the ticket.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.440
First, the purchase
of a lottery ticket

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.050
must be made from within the UK.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.970
This requires that
the application be

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.540
able to geolocate the player.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.530
Luckily, most telcos have a
location API available for use.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.930
Next, the lottery
requires the purchaser

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.890
to be at least 16 years of
age, therefore the application

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.780
must be able to
identify the player

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.050
and look up the player's profile
to determine if the minimum age

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.790
qualifications are met.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.250
This can be done
through an identity API.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.440
Once the minimum
qualifications have been met

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.150
and the player is
ready to purchase,

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.910
the application must
provide the ability to pay.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.520
The billing API
allows the application

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.800
to accept payment and
the player to complete

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.670
the purchase of lottery ticket.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.187
The API product is formed
as a collection of APIs

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.520
that provides the user with
the experience of purchasing

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.490
a lottery ticket and ensuring
that the minimum qualifications

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:47.690
are met.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.510
Purchase must be
made within the UK,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.180
purchaser must be at
least 16 years of age,

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.050
payment can be completed as
part of that transaction.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.180
Furthermore, including
reusable assets

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.180
such as an enterprise security
policy and a traffic management

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.660
policy will ensure adherence
to corporate requirements,

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.270
enabling faster time to market.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.490
Because the lottery
API product packages

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.740
all of the necessary
items, applications

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.720
can be quickly created
to allow purchasing

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.200
of lottery tickets over a
wide variety of experience

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.120
that is convenient
for the player,

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.690
like online, mobile app,
and kiosk purchases.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:25.970
Another example of the
API product mindset

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.030
is with one of our customers
who is the leader in ticket

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.710
sales and distribution.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.340
The company uses APIs to enable
customers to discover events,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.950
such as concerts
or sporting events,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.550
and provides the ability to
purchase tickets on the spot.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.650
This capability is
provided through APIs

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.870
made available to partners
who include the features

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.060
within their applications.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.310
Perhaps you are planning
a weekend getaway

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.110
and want to reserve
a hotel room.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.930
In the process of reserving
a room on the hotel website,

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.650
you might see a link to explore
events within the local area.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.560
The link allows you to
discover events in the area

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.570
and enables you to buy tickets.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.780
A one stop shop to complete
your weekend getaway agenda.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.840
To enable this
experience, API products

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.240
make it easy for partners to
create the user experience.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.560
Let's take a closer look at
this partner API product.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.540
The company's
partner API product

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.180
addresses two key challenges.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.330
How to enable both internal
developers and partner

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.750
developers, and how to
ensure the appropriate level

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.810
of security to stop bots
from circumventing limits

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.600
placed on ticket purchases.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.420
Since security was a
must have, that policy

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.990
was automatically included
in the API product.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.630
In addition, consumers
wanted to find events

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.910
using various search criteria.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.940
Search by artists
such as Beyonce,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.520
search by venue like
the AT&amp;T stadium,

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.550
and search by dates
for the event.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.520
The discovery API had all
of the necessary calls

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.180
to obtain the event data.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.530
Finally, in order to reserve
and purchase the tickets,

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.350
you need a commerce API to
complete the transaction.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.610
This could all be bundled in
a simple to use API product.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.650
Over the course of
its use, the company

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.440
found that the needs of
the partner developer

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.190
were the same as the needs
of the internal developer.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.440
So one API product
could meet the needs

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.690
of both types of API consumers.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.640
Now that you have an
understanding of the API

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.830
product mindset, how do you go
about building an API product?

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.480
Let's dig into this.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.320
Key factors in building
an API product are--

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.280
design your API product
with an outside end mindset.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.030
This requires understanding
your customer and the use case.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:45.330
In the case of the national
lottery focused on the player,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.450
you might take on the
persona as a player

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.880
consumer I want to
purchase a lottery

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.320
ticket while I'm on the go.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.680
For wealth management as
an individual investor,

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.900
I want to view my
account holdings.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.750
Or as a financial
advisor, I want

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.480
to view my client's
account holdings.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.190
Taking a look at
the consumer's view

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.290
informs how to design
your API product

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.130
from the most common use cases.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.160
Next, take a look at the needs
of your application developers

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.410
who will create the
experience for your customers.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.470
They need a way to get started
quickly with your API product.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.170
That's why documentation
is so important,

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.640
but documentation
alone is not enough.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.660
Make it easy for your
developers to discover your APIs

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.240
say through an Apigee
developer portal.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.540
The documentation makes
the APIs accessible.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.730
And finally, review
the analytics

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:37.820
to determine what works well
and what needs improvement,

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.690
then continue to iterate to
improve your API products.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.550
Gone are the field of dreams.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.740
There is so much
information bombarding you

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.280
through the world wide web,
email campaigns, social media,

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.740
and even robocalls to
disturb you that everyone

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:53.930
is on information overload.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.500
In order to get the attention
that your API product deserves,

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.210
you have to market
and evangelize

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.700
your product directly
to your target audience.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.130
Focus on your developer
needs, give them

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:08.030
what they want as far as ease of
use, onboarding, and adoption,

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:10.730
and make it fast
and easy to consume.

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.610
API product mindset means
understand the needs of others

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.240
in the digital value chain,
both upstream and downstream.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.870
The application developer is the
consumer of your API product.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.030
Documentation
describing the product

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.880
is the key to its success.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.790
Likewise, for developers
to get started quickly

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:31.250
with an API product,
you must provide

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.000
foundational information to get
started, such as what data does

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.000
the API product provide,
how to access it,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.100
and what is the
format of the data.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.440
To recap, the API
product mindset

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.960
consists of three
key focus areas.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.730
Focus on the user experience.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.250
Think outside in, and
get a good understanding

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.700
of the user's needs in order to
create a positive experience.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.920
Make it easy to consume with
documentation and sample code.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:58.270
Time to market is key.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.220
Every enterprise company
is jumping on board

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.840
to enable digital assets.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.810
The first to market will have
a leg up on their competitors.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:05.650
Iterate.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:09.610
Don't try to boil the ocean,
but designed for extensibility.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.310
Design for the basic
value proposition,

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:16.460
get feedback, and continue to
iterate to improve the product.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.270
Thanks for your time, and be
sure to watch the next video

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:21.840
to learn about
customer journeys.

